</script>

In [Week 5](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-5/), you saw how ProPublica's ["Dollars for Docs"](https://projects.propublica.org/docdollars/) project drew worldwide attention by turning a massive, obscure dataset of pharmaceutical company payments to doctors into a searchable, browsable website.

Examples of projects like this are everywhere. These include the Washington Post's [police shootings database](https://www.washingtonpost.com/graphics/investigations/police-shootings-database/), USA Today's [NCAA salaries tracker](https://sportsdata.usatoday.com/ncaa/salaries/football/coach) and the college rankings published by [U.S. News and World Report](https://www.usnews.com/best-colleges).

What these tools have in common is a simple pattern: a list page where you can search and filter, and a detail page where you can drill into an individual record. That's what you're going to build today.

### Part 1: Introducing our dataset

We'll be working with citations from the [New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development](https://www.nyc.gov/site/hpd/index.page), known as HPD. When an inspector finds a hazard, the agency issues a notice and the building owner is expected to address the problem.

[The dataset on NYC Open Data](https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5/about_data) contains millions of individual violation records going back years, covering a wide range of hazards, from rodent infestations to broken elevators.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/hpd-open-data.png"
  alt="The HPD Housing Maintenance Code Violations dataset on the NYC Open Data portal, showing a table of violation records"
  chromeTitle="Housing Maintenance Code Violations"
  chromeUrl="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

There are already ways to search and explore this dataset. HPD's [tool](https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information) lets you look up individual buildings.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/hpd-online.png"
  alt="HPD Online search interface showing a 'Find Building Information' hero with tabs for Address, BIN, Registration No., and Borough/Block/Lot"
  chromeTitle="HPD Online"
  chromeUrl="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

[JustFix](https://www.justfix.org/en/) is a non-profit that provides tenant organizers and legal aides with [a tool](https://whoownswhat.justfix.org/en/) to build cases against negligent landlords by merging HPD data with other sources.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/justfix.png"
  alt="JustFix's Who Owns What search interface for finding other buildings a landlord might own in NYC"
  chromeTitle="Who Owns What"
  chromeUrl="https://whoownswhat.justfix.org/en/"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

You'll try to provide a service these sites don't.

You'll narrow your focus to a single hazard, lead paint. It's a hazard that is particularly dangerous for children and worthy of greater focus.

To zoom in even closer, you'll focus on just one borough — the Bronx — which has the highest concentration of housing violations in the city.

Then you'll do something that government sites rarely do: Tally up the total number of open lead paint violations at each building and highlight the most frequent offenders.

[In a separate repository](https://github.com/palewire/nyc-hpd-bronx-lead-paint-violations), I've downloaded the full dataset, applied the necessary filters and rolled up the violations at the building level. I've also merged with the city's [PLUTO](https://www.nyc.gov/content/planning/pages/resources/datasets/mappluto-pluto-change) mapping database to get the latitude and longitude coordinates for each building.

<Screenshot
