## Part 4: Building the list

Now let's display the buildings on the page. Rather than looping over every building, you will start by focusing on the top 20 by open violation count

Once again, we have a pre-made component for this pattern. The [`RankingCard`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-rankingcard--docs) and the parent [`RankingList`](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-rankinglist--docs) components are designed to show ranked lists of items. Like our `DatabaseHeader`, they are documented in Storybook with live previews.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/ranking-list-storybook.png"
  alt="The Storybook documentation page for the RankingList component, showing a live preview of a ranked list with three items and the props table below"
  chromeTitle="RankingList - Storybook"
  chromeUrl="https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/components-rankinglist--docs"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That will work great in this case. Start by importing them into your script block in `+page.svelte`.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3-4"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';
  import RankingList from '$lib/components/RankingList.svelte';
  import RankingCard from '$lib/components/RankingCard.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  console.log(`Loaded ${data.violations.length} buildings`);
  console.log("First record:", data.violations[0]);
</script>

<DatabaseHeader
  headline="Tainted Home Tracker"
  description="The Bronx buildings with the most lead paint violations"
  byline="NYCity News Service"
  date="March 2026"
/>
```

Your goal is to publish a ranking. So you need to sort the buildings by violation count and trim down to the buildings with the most violations. Replace the console logs with this code, which creates a new variable called `top20`.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="8-10"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';
  import RankingList from '$lib/components/RankingList.svelte';
  import RankingCard from '$lib/components/RankingCard.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  let top20 = data.violations
    .sort((a, b) => a.rank - b.rank)
    .slice(0, 20);
</script>

<DatabaseHeader
  headline="Tainted Home Tracker"
  description="The Bronx buildings with the most lead paint violations"
  byline="NYCity News Service"
  date="March 2026"
/>
```


Each record in the JSON already has a `rank` field, so we can use that. The [`.sort()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort) method arranges them in ascending order. Then [`.slice(0, 20)`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/slice) trims the array down to the top 20. This a textbook example of JavaScript's rudimentary data manipulation tools at work.

Now replace everything below your headline with a `RankingList` wrapping an `{#each}` loop that renders a `RankingCard` for each building, just as we've done in your past projects with raw HTML elements.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="20-28"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';
  import RankingList from '$lib/components/RankingList.svelte';
  import RankingCard from '$lib/components/RankingCard.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  let top20 = data.violations
    .sort((a, b) => a.rank - b.rank)
    .slice(0, 20);
</script>

<DatabaseHeader
  headline="Tainted Home Tracker"
  description="The Bronx buildings with the most lead paint violations"
  byline="NYCity News Service"
  date="March 2026"
/>

<RankingList title="Top 20 buildings by open violations">
  {#each top20 as building (building.id)}
    <RankingCard
      rank={building.rank}
      title={building.address}
      value={building.violationCount}
    />
  {/each}
</RankingList>
```

Save and check your browser. You should see a ranked list, but it stretches edge to edge across the full width of the page.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/ranking-list-full-width.png"
  alt="The ranking list stretching edge to edge across the full width of the browser window, with no margin on either side"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5174"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's because, like many components, `RankingList` doesn't impose a maximum width on itself. We need to wrap it in a container that matches the width of the `DatabaseHeader` above it.

Add a `<div>` around the `RankingList` and a `<style>` block at the bottom of `+page.svelte` to constrain it:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="20,30,32-37"}
<script>
  import DatabaseHeader from '$lib/components/DatabaseHeader.svelte';
  import RankingList from '$lib/components/RankingList.svelte';
  import RankingCard from '$lib/components/RankingCard.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();

  let top20 = data.violations
    .sort((a, b) => a.rank - b.rank)
    .slice(0, 20);
</script>

<DatabaseHeader
  headline="Tainted Home Tracker"
  description="The Bronx buildings with the most lead paint violations"
  byline="NYCity News Service"
  date="March 2026"
/>

<div class="container">
  <RankingList title="Top 20 buildings by open violations">
    {#each top20 as building (building.id)}
      <RankingCard
        rank={building.rank}
        title={building.address}
        value={building.violationCount}
      />
    {/each}
  </RankingList>
</div>

<style>
  .container {
    max-width: var(--max-width-wide);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
</style>
```

The `--max-width-wide` variable is defined in the template's global stylesheet at `1200px`. Using it here keeps the list consistent with other wide elements like the `DatabaseHeader`. The `margin: 0 auto` centers the container on the page.

Save again and the list should now sit comfortably within the page layout.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/ranking-list-fixed-width.png"
  alt="The ranking list now constrained to a comfortable width and centered on the page, matching the DatabaseHeader above it"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5174"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

## Part 5: Search and filters

A list of hundreds of buildings isn't very useful without a way to narrow it down. Let's add a search box and a filter dropdown.

We'll use the same reactive tools you learned in [Week 4](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-4/): `$state` for tracking what the user has typed or selected, `$derived` for automatically recalculating the filtered list, and `bind:value` for connecting inputs to state variables.

### Adding a search input

Start by adding a `$state` variable for the search term and a `$derived` variable for the filtered results. Replace the `sorted` variable and swap in our template components:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4-5,7-14"}
<script>
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx_buildings.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import SearchInput from '$lib/components/SearchInput.svelte';

  let search = $state('');

  let filtered = $derived(
    buildings
      .filter(b => b.address.toLowerCase().includes(search.toLowerCase()))
      .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
  );
</script>
```

The `$derived` expression does two things. First, it filters the buildings array to only include those whose address contains the search term. The [`.toLowerCase()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/toLowerCase) calls on both sides make the search case-insensitive, so typing "grand" will match "GRAND CONCOURSE." Second, it sorts the results by violation count, just as before.

Whenever `search` changes — because the user typed something — Svelte automatically re-runs the `$derived` expression and updates everything on the page that depends on it.

Now add a `SearchInput` above the `{#each}` loop. Our template's `SearchInput` component is a styled text field that wires up `bind:value` exactly like a raw `<input>` would:

```svelte
<SearchInput bind:value={search} placeholder="Search by address..." />

<p>{filtered.length} buildings</p>

{#each filtered as building}
  <Card href="{base}/building/{building.id}">
    <h3>{building.address}</h3>
    <p>{building.zip} · {building.violationCount} lead paint violations</p>
  </Card>
{/each}
```

Save and try it. Type an address into the search box and watch the list narrow in real time.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/search-working.png"
  alt="The building list with a search input at the top. The user has typed 'grand' and the list shows only buildings on Grand Concourse."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

## Part 6: Detail pages

So far, everything lives on a single page. But a good database explorer lets you click into individual records and see their full story. That's what we'll build now.

In SvelteKit, every page on your site is a file inside the `src/routes/` folder. Up until now, we've only used `src/routes/+page.svelte`, which is the homepage. But SvelteKit can create pages at any URL by adding folders and files inside `src/routes/`.

We want a separate page for every building, at a URL like `/building/34291`, where the number is the building's ID. To do this, we'll use a SvelteKit feature called [dynamic routes](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/routing#page).

### Creating the route

Create a new folder structure inside `src/routes/`:

```
src/routes/building/[id]/
```

The square brackets around `[id]` are the key. They tell SvelteKit that this part of the URL is a variable — it could be any value. When someone visits `/building/34291`, SvelteKit will load this route and set `id` to `"34291"`.

Inside that folder, create two files. The first is `+page.js`. This is a new type of file we haven't used before. It's called a [load function](https://kit.svelte.dev/docs/load), and its job is to prepare data before the page renders.

Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.js`:

```js
import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx_buildings.json';

export function load({ params }) {
  const building = buildings.find(b => b.id === params.id);
  return { building };
}

export function entries() {
  return buildings.map(b => ({ id: b.id }));
}
```

There are two functions here. Let's take them one at a time.

The `load` function receives a `params` object containing the dynamic parts of the URL. Since our folder is called `[id]`, `params.id` will contain whatever value appears in the URL. We use JavaScript's [`.find()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/find) method to locate the matching building in our dataset, and return it as an object.

The `entries` function serves a different purpose. Because our site is a static site — all the pages are built ahead of time, not generated on-the-fly — SvelteKit needs to know every possible page that could exist. The `entries` function provides that list. It maps over all the buildings and returns an array of objects, each containing an `id` value. When you build the site, SvelteKit will loop through these and generate a separate HTML page for each one.

### Creating the detail page

Now create the page that will display the building's details. Create `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx_buildings.json';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  let { data } = $props();
  let building = data.building;

  let oneYearAgo = new Date();
  oneYearAgo.setFullYear(oneYearAgo.getFullYear() - 1);
  let recentCount = building.violations.filter(v => new Date(v.date) >= oneYearAgo).length;

  let rank = [...buildings]
    .sort((a, b) => b.violationCount - a.violationCount)
    .findIndex(b => b.id === building.id) + 1;
</script>

<a href="{base}/">← Back to all buildings</a>

<HeroTopper
  headline={building.address}
  deck="{building.violationCount} open lead paint violations · ZIP {building.zip}"
  byline="NYC HPD Data"
  date={building.latestDate}
/>

<div class="stats">
  <div class="stat-box">
    <span class="stat-number">{building.violationCount}</span>
    <span class="stat-label">Total open violations</span>
  </div>
  <div class="stat-box">
    <span class="stat-number">{recentCount}</span>
    <span class="stat-label">Issued in the last year</span>
  </div>
  <div class="stat-box">
    <span class="stat-number">#{rank}</span>
    <span class="stat-label">Rank in the Bronx</span>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Violations</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Description</th>
      <th>Date issued</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {#each building.violations as violation}
      <tr>
        <td>{violation.description}</td>
        <td>{violation.date}</td>
      </tr>
    {/each}
  </tbody>
</table>

<MethodologyBox>
  <p>
    Violation records come from the
    <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5" target="_blank">NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development</a>
    via NYC Open Data. Only open lead paint violations are shown.
    For more information about this building, visit
    <a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information" target="_blank">HPD Online</a>.
  </p>
</MethodologyBox>
```

The key line is the first one in the script block: `let { data } = $props()`. When a page has a `+page.js` load function, SvelteKit passes whatever it returns as a `data` prop. So `data.building` is the building object we found in the load function.

The three stat boxes above the table give readers immediate context before they dig into the violation records. `recentCount` filters the violations array to only those issued within the last twelve months, using JavaScript's built-in `Date` object. `rank` imports the full buildings array, sorts it by violation count, and uses [`.findIndex()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/findIndex) to locate this building's position — `+ 1` turns the zero-based index into a human-readable rank.

The violations themselves are now in a `<table>` rather than individual cards. A table is the right tool here: the data is structured, each row has the same two fields, and readers will want to scan down the list. Cards work well for heterogeneous content on list pages; a table is better when every record has the same shape.

Add the styles at the bottom:

```css
.stats {
  display: flex;
  gap: 1.5rem;
  margin: 2rem 0;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

.stat-box {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  padding: 1.5rem;
  background: var(--color-light-gray);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 120px;
}

.stat-number {
  font-size: 3rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--color-dark);
}

.stat-label {
  font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  color: var(--color-medium-gray);
  margin-top: 0.5rem;
  text-align: center;
}

table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  margin: 1rem 0;
}

th, td {
  text-align: left;
  padding: 0.75rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}

th {
  font-weight: bold;
  background: var(--color-light-gray);
}
```

Save the file.

### Linking to detail pages

Because the `Card` component on the list page already renders each entry as a link, the list is wired up automatically. The `href` we passed in Part 4 — `{base}/building/{building.id}` — points directly to the dynamic route we just created. Save and click on any card to test it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/detail-page.png"
  alt="A detail page for a specific Bronx building showing three big-number stat boxes (total violations, violations in the last year, and Bronx rank), followed by a table listing each violation description and date"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Click the "Back to all buildings" link at the top to return to the list. Your search and filter state will reset — that's expected for a static site. Each page load starts fresh.

## Part 7: Adding a locator map

Each building in our dataset has latitude and longitude coordinates. Let's use them to add a small map to the detail page that shows where the building is located.

Our template includes a `LocatorMap` component that embeds an [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/) view centered on any set of coordinates. It uses an iframe — a way to embed one web page inside another — and constructs the URL automatically from the coordinates you pass in. No extra packages required.

Open `src/routes/building/[id]/+page.svelte` and add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="4"}
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx_buildings.json';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import LocatorMap from '$lib/components/LocatorMap.svelte';
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  let { data } = $props();
  let building = data.building;
</script>
```

Place the component after the `HeroTopper` and before the violations list. `LocatorMap` accepts two props — `lat` and `lng` — and renders an OpenStreetMap embed centered on those coordinates with a marker at the exact spot:

```svelte
<LocatorMap lat={building.lat} lng={building.lng} />
```

Save and visit a building's detail page. You should see a small interactive map showing the building's location in the Bronx.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/locator-map.png"
  alt="The detail page now includes a small embedded OpenStreetMap showing a red marker at the building's location, with the surrounding Bronx streets visible"
  chromeTitle="1520 GRAND CONCOURSE"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/building/34291"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

In a couple of weeks, we'll learn how to build full interactive maps from scratch using a library called MapLibre. For now, this simple embed is enough to orient the reader.

## Part 8: Editorial polish

The data is loaded, the search works, and the detail pages are in place. But right now the page is just a database. It needs a frame — a bold editorial header and a source credit at the bottom — to make it a piece of journalism.

ProPublica, The Marshall Project, and other leading newsrooms rarely start a database explorer with a plain `<h1>`. They lead with a designed topper: large type, a contextual summary, a byline, and often a looping illustration that sets the tone before the data appears. Our template includes a `HeroTopper` component that brings this pattern within reach.

### Get a Lottie animation

`HeroTopper` uses [Lottie](https://lottiefiles.com/) — a file format for lightweight vector animations — to display a looping illustration alongside your headline.

Go to [lottiefiles.com](https://lottiefiles.com/) and create a free account. Search for an animation that fits your story. For a lead paint project, something like a building, a child, or a paint brush animation works well. When you find one you like, click "Download" and choose "Lottie JSON." Save the file as `animation.json` in your project's `static/` folder.

### Install lottie-web

`HeroTopper` renders the animation using [lottie-web](https://www.npmjs.com/package/lottie-web), the standard library for playing Lottie files in a browser. Install it in your project:

```bash
npm install lottie-web
```

### Add the hero topper

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
<script>
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  import buildings from '$lib/data/bronx_buildings.json';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
  import SearchInput from '$lib/components/SearchInput.svelte';
  import Dropdown from '$lib/components/Dropdown.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  // ... rest of your script
</script>
```

Replace the `<h1>` at the top of the template with the component:

```svelte
<HeroTopper
  headline="Bronx Buildings with Open Lead Paint Violations"
  deck="Using HPD inspection records to surface unresolved lead paint hazards in New York's most violation-prone borough"
  byline="Your Name"
  date="March 2026"
  lottieSrc="/animation.json"
/>
```

The `deck` prop is a one- or two-sentence summary — think of it as the lede you'd write for a story. `byline` and `date` appear in smaller type below the deck. `lottieSrc` is the path to the JSON file you placed in `static/`.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/hero-topper.png"
  alt="The database explorer with a bold full-width hero header. On the left: a large headline in white type on a dark background, a summary paragraph, and a byline. On the right: a looping Lottie animation of a building."
  chromeTitle="Bronx Buildings with Open Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

### Add a methodology box

Readers trust data journalism more when they can see exactly where the numbers came from. The template includes a `MethodologyBox` component for just this purpose — a structured, consistently styled block that explains the data's origin, how it was processed, and any caveats a reader should know.

Add the import:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2"}
<script>
  import MethodologyBox from '$lib/components/MethodologyBox.svelte';
  import HeroTopper from '$lib/components/HeroTopper.svelte';
  // ... rest of your imports
</script>
```

At the bottom of the page, after the `{#each}` loop, drop in the component with your source information as children:

```svelte
<MethodologyBox>
  <p>
    Data comes from the <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Housing-Development/Housing-Maintenance-Code-Violations/wvxf-dwi5" target="_blank">NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development</a>
    via NYC Open Data. This page shows open lead paint violations for buildings in the Bronx.
    Records were filtered to lead paint violations under Section 27-2056 HMC and aggregated
    at the building level as of March 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    For more information about a specific building's violations, visit
    <a href="https://hpdonline.nyc.gov/hpdonline/provide-information" target="_blank">HPD Online</a>.
  </p>
</MethodologyBox>
```

The component renders an "About the data" box with a bordered left accent and a collapsed/expandable toggle so it does not visually compete with the data itself. You can put any mix of paragraphs, lists, and links between its tags — it accepts open-ended children.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-9/final-page.png"
  alt="The finished database explorer showing the bold hero header at the top, the search and filter controls, the list of building cards, and a methodology box at the bottom"
  chromeTitle="Bronx Buildings with Open Lead Paint Violations"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Save, commit, and push to GitHub. Watch the "Actions" tab to make sure the deploy succeeds, then check your live site.

Take a moment to browse through the data. Click into the buildings at the top of the list and read the violation descriptions. What patterns do you notice? Which neighborhoods have the highest concentrations? That instinct — the impulse to look at the records and ask questions — is what separates a database explorer from just a spreadsheet.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own database explorer

Find a dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/) or another public data source with enough detail per record to support a searchable list page and detail pages. Build a database explorer for it.

Your project must have:

* A searchable or filterable list page with at least two controls (search input, dropdown, toggle — your choice)
* Detail pages for individual records or groups of records
* A `HeroTopper` with a Lottie animation, a headline, a deck, and a byline
* A `MethodologyBox` at the bottom explaining the data source and how it was processed

Some datasets that could work well:

* FDNY inspections by building
* NYC school quality reports and ratings
* Licensed food vendors
* Dog licensing records
* NYPD arrest records by precinct

But you are not limited to NYC. If you find a compelling public dataset from another source, go for it. The point is to find data that has a story in it and build a tool that lets readers find it.

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to share your database explorer with the class and explain how you built it. You should be prepared to:

* Show the dataset you chose and explain why it's interesting
* Walk through your search and filter logic
* Show a detail page and explain how the route works
* Discuss what editorial choices you made — what did you include, what did you leave out, and why
